WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.640
Three constantly-high slackers, a dead-end
telemarketing job, and a whole lot of horrible

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:06.640
people.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.220
Workaholics was one of the best shows about
a group of dudes doing a lot of nothing.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.389
Now that Blake, Adam, and Ders have said goodbye
to Rancho Cucamonga, it's time to take a look

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:17.070
back and everything that made Workaholics
possible.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:22.590
“You gotta / You gotta gotta / You gotta
be fresh.”

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.590
Come together

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.400
A couple of the Workaholics creators have
been making eachother laugh since they were

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.400
little kids.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.689
Kyle Newacheck—the guy who plays drug dealer
Karl on the show—and Blake Anderson grew

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:34.399
up together in northern California.

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:39.690
Their first collaboration was an elementary
school-era comic called Funyun Protectors—about

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.980
anthropomorphic Funyun snack rings who were
also superheroes.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.539
They started performing together in high school
and after graduation moved to Orange County

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:51.769
to attend community college, which is where
they met Adam Devine in an improv class.

00:00:51.769 --> 00:00:55.690
Anders Holm joined the group when he met Devine
at Second City in Los Angeles.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:56.690
The next step?

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:57.949
Making web videos.

00:00:57.949 --> 00:00:59.039
Mail Order Comedy

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:04.280
Back in 2006, they launched their Mail Order
Comedy sketch group, which was fairly successful,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.470
even before they landed on TV.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.130
They had a bunch of comedy shows, did tours,
and were basically going places.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:15.720
Among their creative YouTube ventures and
their own website, they created two MySpace-exclusive

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.909
comedy shows.

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:17.909
Remember MySpace?

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:19.720
If you do, you're probably old.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:23.530
“Hi, my name's Jasmine and I’m on MySpace.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:28.189
I know we’ve never met before, but we should
meet because we live close to one another.

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:29.259
Is this a scam?!”

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:33.939
Once upon a time, it was the Facebook of its
day, so it hosted a lot of exclusive media,

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:38.170
including music, comics, and even web series,
like the ones the Workaholics guys did.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:42.210
"Special Delivery" was a prank show, because
every comic has to get the prank stuff out

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.990
of their system at one point or another.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:48.579
"Crossbows and Mustaches" was an action/comedy
show that combined two of the finest things

00:01:48.579 --> 00:01:50.210
ever created by mankind.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.270
Of course, MySpace couldn't last forever,
so the Mail Order Comedy team went on to make

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.930
bigger and better things, experimenting with
the ideas that would eventually become Workaholics.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:01:59.930
The flophouse

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.870
While taping the first season of Workaholics,
Adam and Blake were convinced that the show

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:04.939
was going to be a flop.

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:09.360
To save money for the rainy days ahead, they
actually lived in the disgusting party house

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.390
where the show was taped, while Anders lived
in a real home with his girlfriend.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.940
Devine and Anderson moved out when the show's
success enabled them to afford proper shelter,

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.800
but Anderson says the old place has become
a flophouse where their friends and family

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:22.800
have crashed.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.940
Anderson's younger brother lived there for
a while, as did Devine's best friend from

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:26.940
high school.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.710
Nevertheless, Devine says the place remains
infested with giant, newborn-sized rats.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:36.910
“There was one like twenty four hour period
that we killed something like 18 rats.”

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:39.800
“Yeah, we called it ‘the night of many
kills.”

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.420
It's Always Sunny in Rancho

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.700
A show about a bunch of terrible people doing
horrible things?

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.590
Of course Workaholics is called an It's Always
Sunny in Philadelphia rip-off, which is basically

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.370
a Seinfeld ripoff anyhow.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:55.630
Workaholics has been called that since before
it even aired, and that was by the creators.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:56.630
Even better?

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:58.150
They said it straight to the cast of Sunny.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.590
According to a MovieWeb interview with Blake,
before the show even aired, the cast of Workaholics

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.190
drunkenly rushed up to the cast of Sunny at
a ComicCon party to tell them that their upcoming

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.130
show was basically a rip-off.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.390
For what it's worth, the Sunny crew reacted
positively, and if there was ever any doubt,

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.260
Sunny's Glenn Howerton cast it aside on Twitter.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.260
Straight Outta Mordor

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.590
The Season 1 episode "Muscle I'd Like to Flex"
begins with a stylishly shot rap video sequence

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:26.900
in which the guys perform a song about being
wizards while dressed in long robes, beards,

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.190
and floppy hats.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.700
“Don’t make this wizard mad, don’t make
this wizard pissed / I could kill a hill giant

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.770
with a flick of my wrist.”

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.840
In the plot of the show, they're rehearsing
for a performance at a Renaissance fair, but

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.160
the wizard rap schtick isn't new.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.660
Under the name The Wizards, they recorded
and released a full album of wizard rap called

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.100
Purple Magic in 2009.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:51.410
In fact, they wanted to pitch a show to Comedy
Central about "gangsta wizards," before a

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:56.320
network executive told them to go with their
other idea, a show about slackers, instead.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.430
Pong gone wrong

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.030
In 2011, Blake Anderson lived out a real-life
event that sounds like it could've happened

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.550
on an episode of Workaholics.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:08.720
At a wild house party in Los Angeles, he raised
the stakes in a game of beer pong by attempting

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.650
to dunk a ping-pong ball... by jumping off
the roof.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.600
The whole thing was captured on video and
ghoulishly sold to TMZ.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.500
“Owww!”

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.479
The accident resulted in a pretty serious
spine fracture.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.720
“Well he broke his back for those of you
that don’t know.”

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:26.720
“Snapped it!”

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.639
“Whoopsies, whoopsie-doozle!”

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:32.080
“He’s got that like, Dr. Octagon spine
now.”

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.080
“I do.”

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.090
Anderson underwent back surgery but made a
full recovery and didn't miss a single episode

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.990
of playing a character who would've done the
same thing.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.379
Arrested Development

00:04:41.379 --> 00:04:46.110
One of the most surprising cameos on Workaholics
came courtesy of Mitch Hurwitz, TV writer

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.229
and creator of Arrested Development.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:51.980
After the first season of Workaholics aired,
he contacted the troupe and told them they

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.879
were "doing great work" and that he'd "like
to be a part of it in any way."

00:04:55.879 --> 00:04:59.990
They offered Hurwitz the opportunity to write
an episode, but he wanted the chance to act—so

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.780
they wrote the part of Cool Eric, the human
resources guy, for him.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:04.780
"I love people.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.259
To say that another way, I LOVE people!

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:10.150
That one’s got a little more fun to it.”

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:14.490
Hurwitz later repaid the favor by casting
the three main stars of Workaholics in cameo

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:19.240
roles as airline employees in the 2013 revival
of Arrested Development.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:20.240
The end

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.229
Despite the fact that Comedy Central probably
would have let the series live forever, Anderson,

00:05:24.229 --> 00:05:28.629
Holm, Devine, and Newacheck all decided that
the seventh season of Workaholics would be

00:05:28.629 --> 00:05:29.629
the last.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:33.240
In a statement, the crew thanked Comedy Central,
its executives, and the show's fans

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.009
"for turning us from Boyz II Men.

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:40.430
It was an incredible run but we've decided
to leave on a HIGH note.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:41.620
Get it?"

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.729
While the show has ended, the team's collaborative
efforts have not.

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:49.210
They're working on an action comedy film called
Game Over, Man for Netflix, and the conclusion

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:52.440
of their show will leave them more time to
focus on bigger projects.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.020
Written by Anders Holm, the movie has been
described as an action-comedy akin to "Die

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.020
Hard in a hotel."

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.220
Which, honestly, sounds pretty fresh.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.220
Thanks for watching!

